  "problem": {
    "id": "train-3370",
    "question": "A watermelon stand sold 46 watermelons. Seventeen customers bought one melon, three customers bought three melons, and the rest bought two melons. How many customers bought two watermelons?",
    "gold": "10",
    "answer": "The customers that bought one watermelon bought 1 * 17 = <<1*17=17>>17 melons.\nThe customers that bought three melons bought 3 * 3 = <<3*3=9>>9 melons.\nThe stand sold 46 - 17 - 9 = <<46-17-9=20>>20 melons to customers who bought 2 melons.\nThus, 20 / 2 = <<20/2=10>>10 customers bought two watermelons.\n#### 10"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine how many customers bought two watermelons, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the total number of watermelons bought by the customers who bought one or three watermelons.\n2. Subtract this number from the total number of watermelons sold to find out how many watermelons were bought by the customers who bought two watermelons.\n3. Determine how many customers bought two watermelons based on the number of watermelons they bought.\n\nLet's start with the first step. The customers who bought one watermelon each bought a total of:\n\\[ 17 \\times 1 = 17 \\text{ watermelons} \\]\n\nThe customers who bought three watermelons each bought a total of:\n\\[ 3 \\times 3 = 9 \\text{ watermelons} \\]\n\nSo, the total number of watermelons bought by the customers who bought one or three watermelons is:\n\\[ 17 + 9 = 26 \\text{ watermelons} \\]\n\nSince the total number of watermelons sold is 46, the number of watermelons bought by the customers who bought two watermelons is:\n\\[ 46 - 26 = 20 \\text{ watermelons} \\]\n\nEach customer who bought two watermelons bought a total of 2 watermelons. Therefore, the number of customers who bought two watermelons is:\n\\[ \\frac{20}{2} = 10 \\]\n\nSo, the number of customers who bought two watermelons is:\n\\[ \\boxed{10} \\]",
